
Goal: Create /etc/motd with exactly 6 lines, line numbers, syntax
highlighting, then save and verify.


STEP 1: Open /etc/motd in vim
-----------------------------
$ sudo vim /etc/motd

(sudo is needed because /etc/ is owned by root)


STEP 2: Enter the 6-line content
--------------------------------
Press  i  to go into INSERT mode, then type:

Line 1: (blank - just press Enter)
Line 2: company name:Google
Line 3: server name:$<hostname>
Line 4: last updated date:9 august 2026
Line 5: "no unauthorized entry"
Line 6: (blank - just press Enter)

Press  Esc  when done.

Result should look like:

(blank)
company name:Google
server name:$<hostname>
last updated date:9 august 2026
"no unauthorized entry"
(blank)


STEP 3: Enable line numbers
---------------------------
Press Esc, then type in command mode:

:set number

Numbers 1 to 6 appear on the left. Confirm you see 1-6.


STEP 4: Enable syntax highlighting
----------------------------------
:set syntax=sh

Colours appear if your terminal supports it.

To make settings permanent, add these lines to ~/.vimrc:
    set number
    syntax on


STEP 5: Save and quit
---------------------
:wq

(w = write/save, q = quit)


STEP 6: Verify line count and content
-------------------------------------
Go to the directory containing the file (e.g. cd /etc), then run:

$ wc -l motd

Expected output:
6 motd

View the file:

$ cat motd


QUICK COMMAND SUMMARY
---------------------
sudo vim /etc/motd     -> open file
i                      -> insert mode
Esc                    -> back to command mode
:set number            -> show line numbers
:set syntax=sh         -> syntax highlighting
:wq                    -> save and quit
wc -l motd             -> count lines (must be 6)
cat motd               -> view file
